WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.640
A ticket printer puts the same digit in two boxes.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.680
Together those digits add to eight,

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.450
so each box shows four.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.580
Turning that ticket would change nothing.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.670
What changes when a ticket carries

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.930
two different digits?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.490
Slide the larger digit from units to tens.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.730
Each extra unit of digit difference

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.570
now gains ten in one place

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:21.980
and loses one in the other.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.000
The net gain is nine.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.900
A reversal that gains thirty six

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.800
therefore moves a digit four larger

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.000
into the tens place.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.080
Our two digits also add to eight.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.440
Set four counters aside for the difference.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.180
The four left split into two matching pairs.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.600
The smaller digit is two and the larger is six.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.870
Print twenty six, then reverse it.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.150
Sixty two minus twenty six is thirty six.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.830
Inspect a different pair of tickets.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.160
A B plus A C ends in zero,

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.940
with hundreds digit B and tens digit D.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.350
All four letters use different digits.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.440
Find the smallest A B.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.310
Two two digit numbers cannot reach two hundred,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.580
so the new hundreds digit is one.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:04.830
B is one.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.520
One plus C must end in zero.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.760
Only nine can complete the ten.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:13.880
Carry one.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.670
The tens total is twice A plus one.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.660
Four would give only nine,

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.650
so the first candidate is five.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.960
Fifty one plus fifty nine equals

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.110
one hundred and ten.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.000
Look at the hundreds and tens.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.450
Two different letters would both be one.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:29.910
Reject that ticket.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.950
Move up one tens digit to six.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.070
Sixty one plus sixty nine

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.800
makes one hundred and thirty.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.750
Read the four letters in order:

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.730
six, one, nine, three.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.210
None collides.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.390
We have checked the only smaller candidate

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.680
that could reach a hundred.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.770
The smallest legal first ticket is sixty one.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.610
A fresh ticket gains twenty seven

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.140
when you reverse its digits.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:55.830
The digits add to eleven.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:56.960
What is the ticket?

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.620
Divide the gain by nine.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.820
The digits differ by three.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.880
Set three aside from eleven,

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.460
then halve the eight left.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.590
The smaller digit is four, the larger seven.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:08.700
Check the journey:

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.060
seventy four minus forty seven

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.280
equals twenty seven.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.160
The ticket gaining value starts

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.260
with the smaller digit.
